Implemented as a **servlet filter** intercepting every request before it reaches the controller:

```java
@Component
public class RateLimitFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final DefaultRedisScript<List> luaScript;

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String apiKey = request.getHeader("X-API-Key");
        try {
            List<Long> result = redis.execute(luaScript,
                List.of("rate_limit:" + apiKey), "100", "1.67",
                String.valueOf(System.currentTimeMillis()));

            response.setHeader("X-RateLimit-Remaining",
                String.valueOf(result.get(1)));
            if (result.get(0) != 1) {
                response.setHeader("Retry-After", "1");
                response.sendError(429, "Rate limit exceeded");
                return;
            }
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Redis error — failing open", e);
            chain.doFilter(request, response); // FAIL OPEN
        }
    }
}
```

**Fail-open vs Fail-closed**: if Redis goes down, **fail-open** (allow traffic) is almost always correct. Fail-closed turns a Redis blip into a total API outage — the protection layer becomes the thing that kills your service. A few seconds of unthrottled traffic while Redis recovers is far better than blocking 100% of legitimate requests.

Pair with: **circuit breaker** (Resilience4j — stop hammering dead Redis), **local fallback counter** (rough per-instance limiting: 5 instances × 20/min each ≈ 100/min total), and **immediate alerting** to ops.
